        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Navajo</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Dinétah, the land within the four sacred mountains: Blanca Peak and Hesperus Mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, Mount Taylor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and the San Francisco Peaks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>; today the Navajo Nation’s reservation of more than 27,325 square miles in the Four Corners region of Arizona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> and New Mexico; more than three-quarters of the Diné live in Arizona and New Mexico.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">A southward migration of Athabaskan peoples from subarctic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> dated to around 1,000 years ago; Spanish contact from the 16th century; Naahondzood, “the fearing time”, the Long Walk and internment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosque-redondo">Bosque Redondo</a>, 1860–1868; the reservation era from the 1868 treaty; the Livestock Reduction, 1933 into the 1950s; the Navajo Nation today.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Diné bizaad, the Navajo language; the clan system, k’é; the hogan; the Blessingway and the Night Chant with their sandpaintings; weaving, from the Chief’s Blankets to trading-post rugs; silverwork, the squash blossom necklace and the concha belt; the Navajo code talkers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Navajo oral tradition and the Diné Bahaneʼ; Spanish records from the 16th century on, among them those of Fray Geronimo de Zarate-Salmeron and Alonso de Benavides; US Army records and treaties; the Meriam Report of 1929; Navajo accounts of the boarding schools; counts by Indian Affairs and the US <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>; histories such as Peter Iverson’s, with Jennifer Denetdale.</span></div>
        </div>
