    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Navajo</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maize.jpg" alt="Maize" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Maize <span class="kid-tale-cr">Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Navajo call themselves Diné, “the people”, and their ancestors are believed to have come south from Canada and Alaska.</p><p>From the Pueblo peoples they learned to farm corn, beans and squash.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotton.jpg" alt="Cotton" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cotton <span class="kid-tale-cr">കാക്കര, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They also learned to weave cotton on upright looms from the Pueblo peoples.</p><p>From the Spanish came sheep and goats, and Navajo women wove the wool into blankets that people valued and traded.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indigo.jpg" alt="Indigo" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Indigo <span class="kid-tale-cr">Photo by Evan Izer (Palladian), CC BY-SA 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Weavers added red yarn and blue indigo dye to their black, grey and white wool, making fine striped blankets.</p><p>Silverwork is an important Navajo art too, with necklaces, bracelets and belts.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosque-redondo.jpg" alt="Bosque Redondo" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Bosque Redondo <span class="kid-tale-cr">JERRYE &amp; ROY KLOTZ MD, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>In 1864 the US Army forced around 9,000 Navajo men, women and children on the Long Walk to Bosque Redondo.</p><p>Many suffered there from disease and cold, and in 1868 the survivors could return to part of their homeland.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/navajo.jpg" alt="Navajo" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Navajo <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>During the Second World War, Navajo code talkers used their own language as a secret code, which the Japanese never cracked.</p><p>Today the Navajo Nation has more than 400,000 citizens and the largest reservation in the United States.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Navajo call themselves Diné, “the people”; their language, Diné bizaad, is Southern Athabaskan, and their ancestors are believed to have migrated from northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>.</li><li>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a> they took up farming and loom weaving, and from the Spanish sheep and goats, which became a currency and a measure of standing in a matrilineal society where women’s families owned the herds, homes and fields.</li><li>In 1864 the US Army forced around 9,000 Navajo on the Long Walk to internment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosque-redondo">Bosque Redondo</a>; the treaty of 1868 let the survivors return to part of their homeland, which grew into the largest reservation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> up to four hundred Navajo code talkers carried messages for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">US Marine Corps</a> in a code built on their own language, in which the word for “turtle” stood for a tank; the Japanese never deciphered it.</p></aside>
    </section>

